 "reading": [
  "Peak breach discharge is 20,454 m\u00b3/s in SPH and 41,510 m\u00b3/s in Delft3D (51 % lower in SPH). SPH resolves the vertical acceleration through the notch in 3-D; Delft3D assumes hydrostatic pressure in a single depth-averaged layer.",
  "Between S1 (0.5 km) and S4 (10 km) the flood peak attenuates by 97 % in SPH and 48 % in Delft3D. Delft3D dissipates energy through Manning bed friction (n = 0.045); SPH has no bed-friction law and loses energy only through artificial viscosity and the particle boundary.",
  "At S4 (10 km) the flood arrives 76.0 min later in SPH (92.0 vs 16.0 min).",
  "The two maximum-extent maps give a critical success index of 0.21 (1 = identical). 0.13 km\u00b2 is flooded only in SPH and 6.69 km\u00b2 only in Delft3D.",
  "Resolution differs by design: SPH uses 12 m particles, the finest spacing this laptop can run for the whole event, so a thin flood layer is only one or two particles deep, while Delft3D computes on 20 m cells. Shallow floodplain edges are therefore the least reliable part of the SPH map."
 ],
